.TH RTRACE 1
.SH NAME
rtrace, ratrace2tl \- draw a timeline of lanes and spans
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rtrace
[
.B -w
]
[
.I file
]
.br
.B aux/ratrace2tl
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rtrace
draws a timeline, in
.IR timeline (6)'s
format, read from
.I file
or its standard input, in its
.IR rio (1)
window, or with
.B -w
in a new one.
Each lane is a row, under its parent and indented by its depth,
with its label at the left.
A faint band shows a lane's life, from its start to its end,
and each span is a mark from its start to its end, colored by its kind.
An axis across the top gives the time from the timeline's earliest,
with ticks in ns, µs, ms or s as the view's width suits;
a legend above it names the kinds by their colors,
and the status line at the foot describes what is under the mouse.
.PP
.I Rtrace
draws only what is in view, and draws spans narrower than a pixel that touch
as one mark, in the color of the longest,
so a timeline of millions of spans stays quick.
.SS Mouse
.TF button\ 1
.TP
button 1
A drag pans the view, in time and down the lanes.
A click on a lane selects it and shows its life,
and a click on the selected lane clears the selection.
.TP
button 3
A menu:
.B timeline
for the lanes and spans,
.B kinds
for a table of the spans by kind and name,
.B tree
for the lanes as a tree,
.B all
to show the whole timeline, and
.BR exit .
.TP
wheel
Over the spans, zooms the view in and out about the mouse;
over the labels, scrolls the lanes.
.PD
.PP
The status line shows the lane under the mouse and the time there,
and, over a span, its kind, name, length and detail.
.SS Keys
.TF Tab
.TP
.B +
Zoom in by a factor of two, about the view's middle, as
.IR trace (1)'s
does.
.TP
.B -
Zoom out by a factor of two.
.TP
Tab
Go to the next view: timeline, kinds, tree, and back to timeline.
.TP
.B q
Delete
Exit.
.PD
.SS Views
The kinds view is a table, a row for each span name,
with its kind's color, its count, its total time and a bar as long as its share of the longest total.
The tree view lists the lanes as they nest, each with its count of spans;
a click on a lane selects it and goes back to the timeline, showing its life.
.SS Colors
The theme
.RI ( theme (6))
gives the colors:
.B back
and
.B text
for the window,
.B ltext
for the axis and the lanes' counts,
.B border
for the rules,
.B rtrace1
to
.B rtrace5
for the kinds, in their order,
.B rtracelife
for the lanes' bands, and
.B rtracesel
for the selected lane.
A sixth kind takes the first color again.
.SS Ratrace2tl
.I Aux/ratrace2tl
reads the output of
.IR ratrace (1),
or the boot trace in
.BR /dev/ratrace ,
from
.I file
or its standard input, and writes a timeline.
Each pid is a lane, nested under the process whose
.B Rfork
returned it, labeled with its pid and the text names it ran under,
joined by
.LR → .
Each call is a span from its start to its stop,
named by the call in lower case, with its arguments and its return, the pointers left out, for detail,
and of one of five kinds:
.B io
(files and I/O) for open, create, close, pread, pwrite, seek, stat, fstat, wstat, fwstat, remove, dup, pipe and fd2path;
.B ns
(name space) for bind, mount, unmount and chdir;
.B proc
(processes) for rfork, exec, exits, await and notify;
.B sync
(waiting) for rendez, sleep and alarm;
and
.B misc
(other) for the rest.
An
.B Exits
is a span of no length at the process's last stop.
Where
.I ratrace
wrote a call's start and end apart,
.I ratrace2tl
gives the end to the latest call still open whose end has that shape,
and leaves out a child's first call, whose start
.I ratrace
does not show.
.SH EXAMPLES
Trace a command and draw its calls:
.IP
.EX
ratrace -c /bin/ls /tmp >[2=1] >/dev/null | aux/ratrace2tl | rtrace
.EE
.PP
Draw the boot so far, in a new window:
.IP
.EX
aux/ratrace2tl /dev/ratrace | rtrace -w
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/rtrace
and
.B src/cmd/ratrace2tl.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR ratrace (1),
.IR trace (1),
.IR timeline (6),
.IR theme (6)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Rtrace
exits with the line number of the first record it cannot read:
a wrong magic or version, a record with too few fields, or a time that is not a number.
.SH BUGS
Plan 9's keyboard gives Tab for Shift-Tab, so Tab only goes forward;
the menu goes to any view.
.I Ratrace2tl
joins a call's start and end by their order and their shape, which processes traced at once
can confuse; the boot trace writes each call whole.
